/// \defgroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig WWMath: Matrix3 rotations, Matrix3D scaling, planes and trig wrappers
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief Standalone copies of a few WWMath header inlines (Matrix3
///        rotations and row constructor, per-axis Matrix3D scaling, a
///        PlaneClass constructor), and the game's global float trig
///        wrappers and tolerance compare.
///
/// Two kinds of code share this group.
///
/// The first is Westwood WWMath code that lives in headers:
/// - Matrix3::Rotate_Y, both overloads, and the free function
///   Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3, both overloads (matrix3.h);
/// - the Matrix3 constructor that takes three rows (matrix3.h);
/// - Matrix3D::Scale with three factors or with a Vector3 (matrix3d.h);
/// - the PlaneClass constructor from a normal and a point (plane.h).
///
/// matrix3.h and matrix3d.h mark these functions WWINLINE, which WWLib's
/// always.h defines as __forceinline under MSVC, and plane.h marks its
/// constructor inline, so code that uses them normally gets its own inlined
/// copy. Retail nevertheless also contains one standalone body of each.
/// A scan of the retail image finds no call to any of these standalone
/// bodies and no stored address of one. Why retail keeps them is not
/// established. The small units under Code/Libraries/Source/WWVegas/WWMath/
/// that hold them only make the compiler emit the header's body; the helper
/// functions these units add for that purpose are not retail code. The guide
/// reads the declarations from the BFME 1 headers under
/// reference/open-bfme-1/game/Libraries/Source/WWVegas/WWMath/, not from
/// the private header copies some units compile against.
///
/// The second kind is the game's own global math helpers in
/// \ref wwmath.cpp "WWMath/wwmath.cpp": Sin, Cos, Tan, ASin, ACos and
/// areApproxEqual. Retail's export table names all six, which is the
/// evidence for their names and parameter types. They are global functions,
/// not members of the WWMath class, whose own Sin and Cos in wwmath.h are
/// different functions. Zero Hour declares the five trig functions in
/// Lib/trig.h and defines them in GameEngine/Source/Common/System/Trig.cpp;
/// neither Zero Hour nor the BFME 1 sources contain areApproxEqual.
///
/// \par Conventions
/// - Matrix3 is three Vector3 rows. operator[] returns row i, so m[i][j] is
///   row i, column j. Points are column vectors: Matrix3::Rotate_Vector
///   takes the dot product of each row with the vector.
/// - Matrix3D is the 3x4 affine transform: three rows of four floats, with
///   the translation in the fourth column. Matrix4 and Vector4 are in
///   \ref grp_w1_wwmath_matrix4_aabox.
/// - Angles are in radians. Each rotation helper comes in two forms: one
///   takes the angle and computes its sine and cosine, the other takes a
///   sine and cosine the caller already has.
/// - The BFME 1 headers use original Generals' class name, Matrix3. Zero
///   Hour's matrix3.h renames the class Matrix3x3; its history line says
///   "Matrix name change to avoid MAX conflicts". The function bodies are
///   otherwise the same in both. No retail symbol confirms either spelling
///   for BFME 2.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing in this group is data-driven. No INI field, map script or other
/// game data reaches these functions directly, and none of them has a
/// tunable or a limit that a mod can change.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Retail computes sines and cosines in two different ways. Sin, Cos and
///   Tan run the x87 FSIN, FCOS and FPTAN instructions directly, and ACos
///   and ASin call the inverse-trig helpers of the Visual C++ 7.1 runtime
///   DLL, msvcr71.dll. None of the five wrappers rounds its result to
///   float: it is left on the x87 stack and is not rounded to float until
///   the caller stores it as one. FSIN, FCOS and FPTAN produce that result
///   in x87 extended precision whatever the control word's precision
///   setting, so the value is whatever the running CPU's FSIN, FCOS or
///   FPTAN computes. Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float) and
///   Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3(float) instead widen the angle to double,
///   call msvcr71.dll's sin and cos, and round each result to float. A sine
///   in retail can therefore come from the CPU or from the runtime DLL,
///   depending on the caller.
/// - GameLogic code calls the trig wrappers. Callers of Sin and Cos include
///   Thing::setOrientation, Locomotor::locoUpdate_moveTowardsAngle,
///   Weapon::computeApproachTarget and ToppleUpdate::applyTopplingForce;
///   ACos is called from Object::GetRelativeAngle and
///   AIGroup::groupRotateFiringarc, ASin from TurretAIAimTurretState::update.
///   Their results feed the lockstep simulation. A build that computes them
///   another way, such as a 64-bit build (64-bit MSVC emits no x87 code), a
///   portable or SSE math library or a different runtime, can differ in the
///   last bits. That is enough to fall out of sync with retail peers and,
///   if BFME 2's replays re-run the simulation as Zero Hour's do, with
///   replays recorded by retail. A port that plays only against itself
///   still needs the same implementation on every peer.
/// - The matrix and plane bodies here do their arithmetic in SSE single
///   precision. PlaneClass::PlaneClass(const Vector3 &, const Vector3 &)
///   sums its dot product in a different order from the header's source;
///   its entry explains why that matters for bit-exact results.


/// \file WWMath/wwmath.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief The global float trig wrappers Sin, Cos, Tan, ASin and ACos, and
///        areApproxEqual.
///
/// Six small global functions that retail exports by name. The five trig
/// wrappers are the game's counterpart of Zero Hour's Lib/trig.h functions,
/// which Zero Hour defines in GameEngine/Source/Common/System/Trig.cpp.
/// That Zero Hour file also holds fixed-point lookup-table versions of
/// Sin, Cos, Tan and ACos, which its DEFAULT_TRIG define switches off; the
/// BFME 2 bodies use no table. Despite the file name, nothing here belongs
/// to the WWMath class.


/// \file WWMath/matrix3.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief Matrix3, the 3x3 rotation matrix, and its free rotation helpers.
///
/// The BFME 1 header this project compiles. Apart from the class's name,
/// its function bodies read the same in Zero Hour's matrix3.h.
/// Nearly everything in it is inline. Besides the class, it defines free
/// functions such as Create_X_Rotation_Matrix3, Create_Y_Rotation_Matrix3
/// and Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3, which return a new rotation matrix, while
/// the member functions Rotate_X, Rotate_Y and Rotate_Z rotate an existing
/// one.


/// \fn void Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float theta)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief Rotates the matrix about the Y axis by an angle in radians,
///        applying the rotation on the right.
///
/// Computes the sine and cosine of theta and then does what
/// Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float, float) does. The header describes it as
/// post-multiplying the matrix with a rotation about Y: the matrix becomes
/// itself times the Y rotation. With column vectors that rotation acts on a
/// point before the existing matrix does, so it turns about the matrix's
/// own local Y axis.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail widens theta to double and calls msvcr71.dll's sin and then its
/// cos, rounding each result to float, and then runs the two-argument
/// overload's arithmetic inline in SSE single precision. It does not use
/// the game's own Sin and Cos.
///
/// \param theta Angle in radians.
/// \see Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float, float), Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3(float)


/// \fn void Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float s, float c)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief Rotates the matrix about the Y axis, given the sine and cosine of
///        the angle.
///
/// The form for callers that already have the sine and cosine, which saves
/// the two trig calls of Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float). It post-multiplies the
/// matrix by the right-handed rotation about Y whose rows are (c, 0, s),
/// (0, 1, 0) and (-s, 0, c).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Each row's X and Z entries are replaced: x becomes c x - s z and z
/// becomes s x + c z, both computed from the old values. The Y column does
/// not change. Nothing checks that s and c belong to one angle; a pair
/// whose squares do not sum to one also scales the X and Z columns.
///
/// \param s Sine of the angle.
/// \param c Cosine of the angle.
/// \see Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float)


/// \fn Matrix3 Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3(float s, float c)
/// \brief Returns a new matrix that rotates about the Z axis, given the
///        sine and cosine of the angle.
///
/// The rows are (c, -s, 0), (s, c, 0) and (0, 0, 1): the right-handed
/// rotation about Z, which for a positive angle turns the X axis towards
/// the Y axis. A free function in matrix3.h, not a member of Matrix3; it
/// builds a fresh matrix where Matrix3::Rotate_Z would rotate an existing
/// one.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The matrix is returned by value: the caller passes a hidden pointer to
/// the result, and the function writes into it directly with no
/// intermediate copy. Retail first default-constructs the result's three
/// rows through the compiler's array-construction helper, whose per-row
/// constructor does nothing, and then stores the nine values. As with the
/// other overloads, nothing checks that s and
/// c belong to one angle.
///
/// \note The name and the matrix layout come from matrix3.h (BFME 1 and
///       Zero Hour). The retail body stores exactly the values above, which
///       supports the name, but no retail symbol spells it.
///
/// \param s Sine of the angle.
/// \param c Cosine of the angle.
/// \return The rotation matrix.
/// \see Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3(float)


/// \fn Matrix3 Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3(float rad)
/// \brief Returns a new matrix that rotates about the Z axis by an angle in
///        radians.
///
/// Computes the sine and cosine of rad and builds the same matrix as
/// Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3(float, float).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail widens rad to double and calls msvcr71.dll's sin and then its
/// cos, rounding each result to float, and then builds the matrix with the
/// two-argument overload's code inline. It does not use the game's own Sin
/// and Cos.
///
/// \note The name comes from matrix3.h, as for the two-argument overload;
///       no retail symbol spells it.
///
/// \param rad Angle in radians.
/// \return The rotation matrix.
/// \see Create_Z_Rotation_Matrix3(float, float), Matrix3::Rotate_Y(float)


/// \fn Matrix3::Matrix3(const Vector3 &v0, const Vector3 &v1, const Vector3 &v2)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief Builds a matrix from three row vectors.
///
/// Copies v0, v1 and v2 into rows 0, 1 and 2. The vectors become rows, not
/// columns: a caller holding three axis vectors that should be the
/// matrix's columns has to transpose the result. The constructor is
/// declared explicit, so it is never used as an implicit conversion.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail's standalone copy first default-constructs the three rows
/// through the compiler's array-construction helper, whose per-row
/// constructor does nothing, then copies each
/// vector's three floats, and returns the object's address as constructors
/// do.
///
/// \param v0 Row 0.
/// \param v1 Row 1.
/// \param v2 Row 2.


/// \fn void Matrix3D::Scale(float x, float y, float z)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief Scales the transform's X, Y and Z axes by separate factors,
///        leaving the translation alone.
///
/// Multiplies the first column of every row by x, the second by y and the
/// third by z. The fourth column, the translation, does not change. This is
/// the same as multiplying the matrix on the right by a diagonal scale
/// matrix, so the scale applies along the transform's own local axes,
/// before its rotation and translation. Retail keeps this overload and
/// the other two, Matrix3D::Scale(float) for a uniform factor and
/// Matrix3D::Scale(Vector3 &), together in the header's order.
///
/// \warning The comment above the Scale functions in Zero Hour's and
///          BFME 1's matrix3d.h warns that the matrix inverse function only
///          works with orthogonal matrices. That holds for
///          Matrix3D::Get_Orthogonal_Inverse, which transposes the rotation
///          part, and for the static Matrix3D::Inverse_Transform_Vector and
///          Matrix3D::Inverse_Rotate_Vector, which the header says give
///          wrong results on a scaled or sheared matrix. Matrix3D::Get_Inverse
///          is a general inverse built on D3DX's D3DXMatrixInverse in Zero
///          Hour and BFME 1, and BFME 2's Get_Inverse calls D3DXMatrixInverse
///          as well, so it can invert a matrix scaled by non-zero factors.
///
/// \param x Factor for the X axis, the first column.
/// \param y Factor for the Y axis, the second column.
/// \param z Factor for the Z axis, the third column.
/// \see Matrix3D::Scale(Vector3 &)


/// \fn void Matrix3D::Scale(Vector3 &scale)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief Scales the transform's axes by the three components of a vector.
///
/// The header implements it as Matrix3D::Scale(float, float, float) with
/// scale's X, Y and Z components, and the retail copy does the same work,
/// with all three components loaded first. The parameter is a non-const
/// reference although the function only reads it, so a const Vector3
/// cannot be passed.
///
/// \warning As for the three-factor overload: after scaling, invert with
///          Matrix3D::Get_Inverse, not Matrix3D::Get_Orthogonal_Inverse or
///          the static Inverse_Transform_Vector and Inverse_Rotate_Vector,
///          which assume an orthogonal matrix.
///
/// \param scale Per-axis factors: X for the first column, Y for the second,
///              Z for the third. Read only.
/// \see Matrix3D::Scale(float, float, float)


/// \fn PlaneClass::PlaneClass(const Vector3 &normal, const Vector3 &point)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_wwmath_rotation_trig
/// \brief Builds the plane with a given normal that passes through a given
///        point.
///
/// PlaneClass stores a plane as a normal N and a distance D, and a point p
/// lies on the plane when the dot product of N and p equals D. plane.h
/// warns that this D has the opposite sign from the D in the familiar
/// Ax + By + Cz + D = 0 form. This constructor copies normal into N and
/// sets D to the dot product of normal and point.
///
/// The normal is stored as given; nothing normalises it. With a normal that
/// is not unit length the plane still passes through point, but D is then
/// scaled by the normal's length and is no longer the distance from the
/// origin.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail adds the Y and Z products first and the X product last, although
/// Vector3::Dot_Product in the header writes them in X, Y, Z order. The
/// reconstruction reproduces retail from the unchanged header, so the order
/// is the compiler's. Float addition is not associative, so a rebuild with
/// a compiler that keeps the source order can differ in the last bit.
///
/// \param normal Plane normal, used as given.
/// \param point  Any point on the plane.


/// \fn bool areApproxEqual(float a, float b, float tolerance)
/// \brief Tests whether two floats differ by strictly less than a
///        tolerance.
///
/// Returns true when a - b lies strictly between -tolerance and tolerance.
/// The test is strict at both ends, so:
/// - a tolerance of zero or less always gives false, even when a equals b;
/// - a NaN in any argument gives false;
/// - a difference that overflows to infinity gives false.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The difference is computed once in SSE single precision and compared
/// with tolerance and with its negation. No call to it and no stored
/// address of it was found in the retail image; retail's export table
/// names it.
///
/// \param a         First value.
/// \param b         Second value.
/// \param tolerance Exclusive bound on the difference; should be positive.
/// \return true if the difference is strictly inside the band.


/// \fn float Sin(float value)
/// \brief Sine of an angle in radians, from the x87 FSIN instruction.
///
/// A thin wrapper: it loads value onto the x87 stack, runs FSIN and
/// returns. The result stays on the x87 stack in x87 extended precision;
/// it is not rounded to float until the caller stores it as one. FSIN
/// only accepts arguments of magnitude below 2 to the 63rd power and
/// returns larger ones unchanged.
///
/// Retail callers include GameLogic code such as Thing::setOrientation,
/// Thing::getUnitDirectionVector2D, Locomotor::locoUpdate_moveTowardsAngle,
/// Weapon::computeApproachTarget and ToppleUpdate::applyTopplingForce, and
/// client code such as W3DRopeDraw::buildSegments and
/// SwayClientUpdate::updateSway. The float overload of sin in the C++
/// math header compiles to the same bytes, and the linker folded the two
/// into one body, so a call to it does not show which of the two names the
/// caller's source used.
///
/// Zero Hour's Sin returns sinf(x). The Trig.cpp it lives in also holds a
/// fixed-point lookup-table version that its DEFAULT_TRIG define switches
/// off; a comment there says the table was meant for speed and
/// repeatability across machines.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The result is the CPU's FSIN result. See the group's Remastering notes
/// for what changing it does to lockstep play.
///
/// \param value Angle in radians.
/// \return The sine, in x87 extended precision.
/// \see Cos, Tan, ASin


/// \fn float Cos(float value)
/// \brief Cosine of an angle in radians, from the x87 FCOS instruction.
///
/// The same wrapper as Sin with FCOS in place of FSIN, with the same range
/// limit and the same unrounded result. Retail callers include
/// Thing::setOrientation, Thing::getUnitDirectionVector2D,
/// Locomotor::locoUpdate_moveTowardsAngle, Weapon::computeApproachTarget,
/// ToppleUpdate::applyTopplingForce, W3DRopeDraw::buildSegments and
/// SwayClientUpdate::updateSway. As with Sin, the C++ math header's float
/// overload of cos is folded onto the same body. Zero Hour's Cos returns
/// cosf(x).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The result is the CPU's FCOS result; see the group's Remastering notes.
///
/// \param value Angle in radians.
/// \return The cosine, in x87 extended precision.
/// \see Sin, ACos


/// \fn float Tan(float value)
/// \brief Tangent of an angle in radians, from the x87 FPTAN instruction.
///
/// FPTAN leaves the tangent on the x87 stack with the constant 1.0 pushed
/// on top of it; the wrapper pops the 1.0 and returns the tangent, again
/// unrounded. No call to it and no stored address of it was found in the
/// retail image; retail's export table names it. Zero Hour's Tan returns
/// tanf(x).
///
/// \param value Angle in radians.
/// \return The tangent, in x87 extended precision.
/// \see Sin, Cos


/// \fn float ASin(float value)
/// \brief Arc sine, through the Visual C++ 7.1 runtime's x87 helper.
///
/// Loads value onto the x87 stack and calls the arc-sine helper that
/// msvcr71.dll exports for compiler intrinsics, which takes its argument
/// and leaves its result on the x87 stack. The result is not rounded to
/// float before returning. Retail callers include
/// TurretAIAimTurretState::update. Zero Hour's ASin returns asinf(x); the
/// BFME 1 reconstruction's returns asin(x) cast to float.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The result comes from the msvcr71.dll in use, not from the CPU alone.
///
/// \param value Sine value, normally between -1 and 1.
/// \return The angle in radians, not rounded to float.
/// \see ACos, Sin


/// \fn float ACos(float value)
/// \brief Arc cosine, through the Visual C++ 7.1 runtime's x87 helper.
///
/// The same wrapper as ASin, calling msvcr71.dll's arc-cosine helper.
/// Retail callers include Coord2D::toAngle, Object::GetRelativeAngle,
/// AIGroup::groupRotateFiringarc and AIGroup::moveFormationToPos. Zero
/// Hour's ACos returns acosf(x) under DEFAULT_TRIG, the define that also
/// switches off its lookup-table version.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The result comes from the msvcr71.dll in use; see the group's
/// Remastering notes.
///
/// \param value Cosine value, normally between -1 and 1.
/// \return The angle in radians, between 0 and pi, not rounded to float.
/// \see ASin, Cos
